21.2 Put tables inside a column
nest() from tidyr takes a table and returns an outer table containing a list column of inner tables. .by = region identifies the column to keep outside: one outer row represents each observed region. The other columns move together into an inner table, stored in a list column named data:

nested_schooling &lt;- schooling |&gt;
  nest(.by = region)
nested_schooling
# A tibble: 4 × 2
  region    data                
  &lt;fct&gt;     &lt;list&gt;              
1 northeast &lt;tibble [616 × 3]&gt;  
2 west      &lt;tibble [894 × 3]&gt;  
3 midwest   &lt;tibble [923 × 3]&gt;  
4 south     &lt;tibble [1,553 × 3]&gt;


The result has 4 outer rows and two columns. region identifies the container. Each cell in data holds a tibble with respondent rows and the three columns id, educ, and maeduc. The printed &lt;tibble [...]&gt; abbreviation describes a stored table’s dimensions, rather than a text value. Read the row and column counts in this output for each region.
Nesting has changed the organization of the records. It has not replaced respondents with regional averages. All their IDs and schooling values remain inside the stored tables. This differs from a grouped summary, which returns calculated results and does not retain all the underlying respondent records in its output. It also differs from group_by(), which leaves respondent rows on the outside and attaches a grouping instruction.
The region factor keeps its existing levels. We neither recode the regions nor give them a new order. Outer rows follow the groups encountered in the input; use the region labels to identify them rather than assuming that a row position has a permanent geographic meaning. An unused factor level is a possible category, but has no respondent records to supply an inner table here.
If some respondents had an unknown region, nest(.by = region) would retain them together in an outer row with NA as its key. That container would represent unknown region, not a fifth geographic region. This extract has no such records. Missing educ or maeduc values remain in their respondents’ inner rows regardless of grouping.

25.6 Worked answers






NoteCompare probability predictions





Restore the saved data independently:

library(tidyverse)
library(tidystata)
answer_gss24 &lt;- readRDS("gss24.rds")


answer_model_data &lt;- answer_gss24 |&gt;
  select(id, educ, maeduc, age, sex, degree) |&gt;
  filter(!is.na(id), !is.na(educ), !is.na(maeduc), !is.na(age),
         !is.na(sex), !is.na(degree)) |&gt;
  mutate(
    sex = factor(sex, levels = c("male", "female")),
    college = if_else(degree %in% c("bachelor's", "graduate"), 1, 0)
  )
answer_logit &lt;- stats::glm(
  college ~ maeduc + age + sex, data = answer_model_data,
  family = stats::binomial(link = "logit")
)
answer_probit &lt;- stats::glm(
  college ~ maeduc + age + sex, data = answer_model_data,
  family = stats::binomial(link = "probit")
)
answer_model_data |&gt;
  filter(age == 50, sex == "female", maeduc %in% c(12, 16)) |&gt;
  count(maeduc, age, sex)
# A tibble: 2 × 4
  maeduc   age sex        n
   &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;fct&gt;  &lt;int&gt;
1     12    50 female    11
2     16    50 female     2

answer_cases &lt;- tibble(
  maeduc = c(12, 16), age = c(50, 50),
  sex = factor(c("female", "female"), levels = c("male", "female"))
)
answer_predictions &lt;- answer_cases |&gt;
  mutate(
    logit_percent = 100 * stats::predict(
      answer_logit, newdata = answer_cases, type = "response"
    ),
    probit_percent = 100 * stats::predict(
      answer_probit, newdata = answer_cases, type = "response"
    )
  )
answer_predictions
# A tibble: 2 × 5
  maeduc   age sex    logit_percent probit_percent
   &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;dbl&gt; &lt;fct&gt;          &lt;dbl&gt;          &lt;dbl&gt;
1     12    50 female          37.2           37.6
2     16    50 female          55.5           54.8


Logit predicts 37.2% and 55.5% college attainment; probit predicts 37.6% and 54.8%. Each comparison holds modeled age at 50 and sex at female, with four years’ difference in parent schooling. The logit difference is 18.2 percentage points; the probit difference is 17.2. The links use different coefficient scales but give similar probability contrasts here. Both combinations are observed, although sparsely; the predictions pool the model’s entire sample rather than averaging those cells. They remain model-dependent point predictions, with no causal interpretation or survey-design correction.
